It encompasses all things that are considered plants.	凡屬植物之類，皆涵蓋於此。
It refers to anything that is an animal.	凡屬動物之類，皆涵蓋在內。
In this place, it would not surprise me to hear a statue talk, but this one shows no interest in speech as yet.	在這種地方，就算聽見石像開口說話，我也不會意外，但這一尊至今尚無開口的跡象。
You have passed this test and the statue is silent.	你已通過此試煉，石像陷入沉默。
You are not making sense, Arthur.	你這話毫無道理，亞瑟。
It has turned to stone and become part of the statue.	它已化為石頭，成了石像的一部分。
It is part of the statue.	它是石像的一部分。
What nonsense is this? You were given the dove.	這是什麼胡言亂語？你已得到了那隻鴿子。
That is quite beyond your ability.	這遠超出你的能力。
You have given the statue the only apple it required.	你已把石像所需的唯一一顆蘋果交給了它。
You have both a green apple and a golden apple.	你身上同時有一顆青蘋果與一顆金蘋果。
The apple fit perfectly in the statue's hand, but caused nothing to happen.	蘋果恰好嵌入石像的手中，卻沒有任何動靜。
You have brought the gift by which I am made complete. For this, I will tell you what you must do to escape the catacombs.	你帶來了使我完整的禮物。為此，我將告訴你該如何逃出這地下墓窖。
First, be warned. From this moment on, every door within the catacombs is a possible trap. If you leave the safety of this room too soon, you may be trapped forever within a tomb.	首先，容我提醒你：自此刻起，這地下墓窖裡的每一扇門都可能是陷阱。若你過早離開這房間的庇護，你或許將永遠困死於某座墓穴之中。
It is Aphrodite's will that you should know something of her history. I will pose you six questions to test your knowledge of her.	阿芙羅黛蒂的旨意，是要你知曉她的一些過往。我將提出六個問題，考驗你對她的認識。
Each question requires but one word in answer. When you have answered all six, I shall reveal to you how you may escape the catacombs.	每個問題只需一個字作答。待你答完全部六題，我便會告訴你如何逃出這地下墓窖。
As for the sacred cup, it lies beyond the catacombs. You will not find it here. The test now begins.	至於那神聖之杯，它在地下墓窖之外，你在此處是找不到的。試煉現在開始。
That is not the answer. The test continues.	這不是答案。試煉繼續。
The answer requires but a single word. The test continues.	答案只需一個字。試煉繼續。
That is the answer. The test continues.	這正是答案。試煉繼續。
You have given the six answers I require and earned your chance at freedom. First, accept this gift.	你已答出我所要求的六個答案，贏得了重獲自由的機會。且先收下這件禮物。
When you have proven yourself by sword and shield, the time will be right to use the dove.	待你以劍與盾證明自己之時，便是使用那隻鴿子的時機。
To escape from the catacombs, you must enter six doors exactly as I tell you. If you choose a door that is wrong, you will find yourself trapped forever.	要逃出這地下墓窖，你必須依我所言，一絲不差地走過六扇門。若你選錯了門，你將永遠受困於此。
You begin here by choosing the first door you must enter.	你要從此處開始，選出你該走的第一扇門。
The doors you choose must face the direction I give you and you must ENTER them in exactly this order....	你所選的門，必須朝向我告訴你的方向，且必須依照這個順序逐一進入……
West, east, south, east, south, north.	西、東、南、東、南、北。
Have no fear for Galahad. When he is healed, I shall set him free. You have earned that boon.	無須為葛拉漢憂心。待他傷勢痊癒，我便會釋放他。你已贏得這份恩典。
I shall not speak again. Your escape relies now upon your own ingenuity.	我不會再開口了。你能否脫身，如今全憑你自己的機智。
You are enclosed in a narrow tunnel within the wall. Inch forward carefully and test the floor as you go.	你被困在牆內一條狹窄的通道中。小心翼翼地一寸寸前進，邊走邊試探地面。
It takes you in one direction. Let us hope you find an exit soon, for it is as cold as the grave and the stone walls covered with something slick and unpleasant.	它只通往一個方向。但願你能盡快找到出口，這裡冷得如同墳墓，石牆上還覆著一層濕滑黏膩的東西。
The tunnel stops at a wall. Push, Arthur! Aye, it gives way into one of the rooms of the catacombs through a hidden doorway.	通道盡頭是一堵牆。用力推，亞瑟！沒錯，它讓開了一道路，通向地下墓窖裡的一間密室，門就藏在其中。
Catch him, Arthur! He must not escape!	抓住他，亞瑟！絕不能讓他逃了！
Hurry! You must not let him out of your sight for an instant!	快！你片刻都不能讓他離開你的視線！
You will never see it again unless you catch that Thief!	除非你抓住那個小偷，否則你再也見不到它了！
Arthur, nothing else matters except that you catch that thief and retrieve the Grail at once!	亞瑟，此刻別無他事要緊，你只須立刻抓住那個小偷，奪回聖杯！
First, you must catch him!	你得先抓住他！
Do not stand there then! Chase him!	那就別杵在那裡！追上去！
First you must catch him and quickly!	你得先抓住他，快！
Aye, you must, but only if you catch the Thief!	正是，但前提是你得先抓住那小偷！
First catch the Thief, then decide how to deal with him.	先抓住那小偷，再來決定如何處置他。
I can hardly blame you for being upset, but it will take more than foul language to catch the thief.	你心煩意亂，我不怪你，但光靠粗言穢語可抓不住那小偷。
Each broken pillar looks much the same as its neighbor. You must find the right one by your wits.	每根斷裂的柱子看起來都與鄰旁的相差無幾。你得憑自己的智慧找出正確的那一根。
The elegant marble pillars which once held a roof have all been broken and thrown down, the work of those who would destroy the worship of Aphrodite.	這些昔日支撐屋頂的華美大理石柱，如今已全數斷裂倒塌，正是那些意圖摧毀阿芙羅黛蒂信仰之人所為。
They are common weeds, of no interest.	那些只是尋常的雜草，沒什麼好留意的。
The round pedestal in the center of the temple's ruins must have once held an altar or statue, but is empty now.	神殿廢墟中央的圓形底座，想必昔日曾立有祭壇或雕像，如今卻已空無一物。
The walls which encompass these ruins have deliberately been build without windows or doors, to enclose and hide this ruin.	圍繞這片廢墟的牆壁，是刻意建成沒有窗戶或門的模樣，為的是封閉並隱藏這座遺跡。
It closed solidly behind you. Only the Goddess herself could reopen it.	它在你身後牢牢闔上了。唯有女神本人才能將它重新開啟。
The stairs bring you to the surface and into blessed light and fresh air.	這階梯將你帶回地面，重見祝福般的光明與新鮮空氣。
Around you is the ruin of a circular temple. Once there was a famous Temple of Aphrodite in Jerusalem. Perhaps this was it.	你身處一座圓形神殿的廢墟之中。耶路撒冷曾有一座著名的阿芙羅黛蒂神殿，或許眼前這座正是。
The marble stones are ancient and weather-worn. The marble pillars are toppled and an empty round pedestal in the middle may have once held a statue or altar.	這些大理石早已古老斑駁，飽經風霜。大理石柱已然傾倒，中央那座空的圓形底座，想必昔日曾立有雕像或祭壇。
Walls without doors or windows have been built entirely around the temple, right up to the very edge of the ruins.	沒有門窗的牆壁將神殿團團圍住，一路築到廢墟的邊緣。
The indentation once held a pillar.	這處凹痕，昔日曾立著一根柱子。
The spiral carved into the threshold is lunar. That is, it turns to the left and denotes feminine power and influence.	門檻上刻著的螺形紋飾屬於月相，也就是說，它向左旋繞，象徵女性的力量與影響。
The steps lead down into a narrow alley.	階梯向下通往一條狹窄的巷弄。
The Thief ran this way. Catch him!	那小偷是往這個方向逃的。抓住他！
Stop babbling and catch the Thief!	別再喋喋不休了，快去抓住那小偷！
He is dead, you fool! Catch the Thief!	他已經死了，你這個傻子！去抓那小偷！
She is gone, you fool! Catch the thief!	她已經走了，你這個傻子！去抓那小偷！
Forget that! Catch the Thief!	別管那個了！去抓那小偷！
The alley is bounded by tall buildings without windows or doors. It takes a sharp turn not far from the Temple stairs.	這條巷弄兩側是沒有窗戶或門的高聳建築。離神殿階梯不遠處，巷弄陡然一轉。
Just around the bend in the alley, there is a tiny grate in the bottom of the wall. Otherwise, there seem to be no windows or doors in these walls.	就在巷弄轉角過去不遠處，牆根有一個小小的柵格。除此之外，這些牆上似乎再無窗戶或門。
The fallen pillars are too heavy to look under.	倒塌的柱子太過沉重，無法翻看底下。
Why do you waste time here? The Temple awaits you but a few steps away.	你何必在此浪費時間？神殿只在幾步之外等著你。
This appears to be the only way in or out of the Temple.	這似乎是進出神殿唯一的通道。
They are the fallen marble pillars that once formed the Temple of Aphrodite.	那些是倒塌的大理石柱，昔日曾構成阿芙羅黛蒂神殿的一部分。
There is none here.	此處並無此物。
There is a tiny grate or barred opening of some sort at the base of this wall.	牆根處有一個小小的柵格，或說某種帶柵欄的開口。
There are a few trees rising above the high walls that encircle the Temple.	幾株樹木高過圍繞神殿的高牆，探出頭來。
A few roofs can be glimpsed in the distance, for all the good it does you.	遠處隱約可見幾片屋頂，但對你也沒什麼用處。
The stairs and only entrance to the Temple face the alley where once they must have faced a major street.	神殿的階梯，也是唯一的入口，如今面向這條巷弄——想必昔日曾面向一條大街。
It is one of the walls which enclose the temple.	這是圍住神殿的其中一面牆。
Aye, that IS your mission. Do get on with it.	正是，這正是你的使命。快去做吧。
He grovels before you, pleading for mercy. You have two choices--take up the Grail and show him mercy, or draw your sword and strike him down.	他在你面前匍匐哀求，乞求饒恕。你有兩個選擇——拿起聖杯，饒他一命，或是拔劍將他斬殺。
She is no longer concerned with these affairs. You must decide whether or not to show mercy to this man.	她已不再過問這些俗事。你必須自行決定是否饒恕此人。
The Thief offers it to you. You must decide whether to slay him, or take it and show him mercy.	那小偷將它奉還於你。你必須決定，是要殺了他，還是收下它、饒他一命。
Arthur, this man is begging for mercy. You must deal with him. Nothing else matters.	亞瑟，這人正在乞求饒恕。你必須處置他，此刻別無他事要緊。
Are you blind? He grovels in front of you.	你瞎了嗎？他就匍匐在你面前。
He deserves nothing from you except possibly your mercy. Either take the Grail or slay him.	他不配從你這裡得到什麼，或許唯有你的憐憫例外。拿起聖杯，或是殺了他，二者擇一。
Do not be absurd. Either take the Grail and show him mercy, or draw your sword and slay him.	休得荒唐。拿起聖杯饒他一命，或是拔劍將他殺了。
The alley ends here, going nowhere. All the other walls around you are featureless.	巷弄到此為止，再無去路。四周其餘的牆面皆平淡無奇。
But to one side there is an alcove in which rests the weathered head of an ancient stone statue. And there is a window at the top of the alcove and another tiny grate at the bottom.	但一側有個壁龕，裡頭安放著一顆風化的古代石像頭。壁龕頂部有一扇窗，底部則另有一個小柵格。
Why do you waste time here? Return to the Temple.	你何必在此浪費時間？回神殿去吧。
Since this is a dead end, you seem to have no choice but to return to the Temple.	既然此處已是死路，你似乎除了回神殿之外別無選擇。
There is a tiny grate or barred opening of some sort at the base of this wall. I believe it may even be the top of a buried window.	這面牆根處有一個小小的柵格，或說某種帶柵欄的開口。我猜這或許正是一扇被掩埋的窗戶的上緣。
And there is a small window high in this alcove, with a single metal bar across it.	壁龕高處還有一扇小窗，橫著一根鐵條。
There is a small window high in this alcove, with a single metal bar across it.	壁龕高處有一扇小窗，橫著一根鐵條。
The stairs and only entrance to the Temple are at the other end of this alley which ends here.	神殿的階梯，也是唯一的入口，位於這條巷弄的另一端，而巷弄到此為止。
It could be the head of a god, a king, a warrior. Who can say? Perhaps the entire statue is buried. We shall never know its strange story.	那或許是神祇之首，或是君王，或是戰士，誰能說得準？也許整尊石像都埋在地底。它的離奇來歷，我們終究無從得知。
That would be wonderful, if it were here.	若真在此處，那就太好了。
That is quite impossible for it is partially buried and would weigh far more than you could manage to move.	這絕無可能，它有一部分埋在地底，重量遠非你所能搬動。
I hardly think such barbaric behaviour is called for.	我實在不認為有必要做出如此野蠻的舉動。
The window is too high to reach.	那扇窗太高，搆不著。
Nay, your god smiles upon you! His rope has broken!	不，你的神明正對你微笑！他的繩子斷了！
By all that is holy, you have dallied shamelessly and the Thief has escaped. There is no hope for you, Arthur. You may as well fall upon your sword.	憑一切神聖之名，你竟如此無恥地虛耗光陰，讓那小偷逃了。你已無望可言，亞瑟。你不如伏劍自盡算了。
By all the gods, he will escape!	憑諸神之名，他要逃了！
Mercy, master, mercy! I am only a poor, miserable wretch! Have mercy!	饒命啊，主人，饒命！我只是個可憐悲慘的窮鬼！求你饒命！
Here is your treasure, untouched! Take it and let me live, I beg you! Have mercy!	這是你的寶物，分毫未動！收下它，饒我一命，我求你了！求你饒命！
He is a pathetic creature. You must choose, Arthur. Either take up the Grail now in an act of mercy....or draw your sword and slay him.	他是個可悲的傢伙。你必須做出抉擇，亞瑟。此刻，或是拿起聖杯，展現憐憫……或是拔劍將他斬殺。
By the gods, he dared to stab you in the back! If not for your mail tunic, you would be dead at the hands of this foul dog.	憑諸神之名，他竟敢從背後刺你一劍！若非你身上這件鎖甲，你早已死在這條惡狗手裡。
It is time to let the Grail decide his fate.	是時候讓聖杯來決定他的命運了。
And so, he comes to the end he deserves.	於是，他迎來了自己應得的結局。
Now you must move quickly to find Galahad and return to Camelot.	如今你必須盡快找到葛拉漢，返回肯萊特。
I do not blame you for slaying him after all the grief he has caused you. The merchants of Jerusalem would no doubt thank you.	他帶給你的苦楚如此之多，我不怪你殺了他。耶路撒冷的商人們必定會感謝你。
At least the Grail is unharmed. Now you must move quickly to find Galahad and return to Camelot.	至少聖杯毫髮無損。如今你必須盡快找到葛拉漢，返回肯萊特。
  Mercy, I beg you, show mercy!	  饒命，我求你，饒了我吧！
The scholar may know more about symbols than you.	這位學者對符號的了解，或許比你更多。
There are no olives.	沒有橄欖了。
Hazm is not here.	哈慈不在這裡。
Perhaps you should stay and seek knowledge from the old scholar.	或許你該留下，向這位老學者請益。
His clothing and bearing are simple, yet his words reveal wisdom and complexity.  There is no doubt that he is what he says he is.	他的衣著與舉止都樸素無華，言語間卻透著智慧與深意。毫無疑問，他確如自己所言。
Even his name, Al-Sirat, is a clue.  It is a name for the knife-edged path of truth that one must tread to reach paradise or heaven.	就連他的名字 Al-Sirat 也是條線索。那是真理之路的名字——一條刃薄如鋒的道路，人須踏過它才能抵達樂園或天堂。
Which means, Arthur, that he invited you here for a specific purpose, one which you must unravel for yourself.	這意味著，亞瑟，他邀你前來自有特定用意，而那用意，你得自己解開。
You must unravel his purpose for yourself.	他的用意，你得自己解開。
The walls are a bit too high for that.	那些牆太高了，做不到。
Overhead, it is open sky.	頭頂上是一片開闊的天空。
Beneath the colourful mats there is pale, sandy earth.	這些色彩繽紛的墊子底下，是淡色的沙質地面。
I suspect this is a place of meditation and that Al-Sirat's home is elsewhere.	我猜這裡是個沉思冥想之處，Al-Sirat 的住所應在別處。
There is nothing here save four bare walls, plants, the two rugs and a small table with a carafe, cups and a bowl of figs.	此處空無一物，只有四面光禿的牆、幾株植物、兩張地毯，還有一張小桌，上頭擺著一個玻璃瓶、幾只杯子與一碗無花果。
Through the open doorway one can see the hills and the high desert.	透過敞開的門，可以望見丘陵與高地沙漠。
The serenity of the scholar's courtyard make the port and town of Gaza seem far away.	這學者庭院裡的靜謐，讓加薩的港口與城鎮彷彿遙不可及。
A tree branch gives some scant shade to this open yard.	一根樹枝為這片開闊的庭院投下些許稀疏的樹蔭。
It is a small cup.  The carafe must contain a strong drink.	這是個小杯子。玻璃瓶裡裝的想必是烈性的飲品。
There is no more qahwah in the carafe.	玻璃瓶裡已沒有 qahwah 了。
The elegant carafe holds the brew, qahwah, that he prepared for you.  From the small size of the cups, it must be quite potent.	這只精緻的玻璃瓶裡，裝著他為你準備的飲品 qahwah。從杯子的小巧看來，這飲品想必後勁十足。
There are no more ripe figs in the bowl.	碗裡已沒有熟透的無花果了。
There are ripe figs in the bowl.	碗裡有熟透的無花果。
Aye, he has aloe vera growing here.  It is a desert plant with a bitter, astringent sap.	沒錯，他在這裡種著蘆薈。這是一種沙漠植物，汁液又苦又澀。
It is a low, round, wooden table just large enough to hold the carafe, cups and one bowl.	這是一張低矮的圓形木桌，大小恰好能擺下玻璃瓶、杯子與一只碗。
It could be a young olive tree in the planter.  Another tree gives some scant shade to this open yard.	花盆裡種的或許是一株幼橄欖樹。另一棵樹則為這片開闊的庭院投下些許稀疏的樹蔭。
They seem to be quite ordinary bushes. I believe that is a young tree in the planter.	這些看起來只是尋常的灌木。我想花盆裡那株是幼樹。
